#edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c4/c4a491847b4a6b647032831e563fe7d4007e63fb5dd4b594dd825c1055a82451.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186702922} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f953bb7-faa5-5d66-9219-34da76defe66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186702929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "66fa83fe-3327-5ef3-953e-2925c58b3039" :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-1+\\frac{1}{3}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-3$ and $x=3$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-3$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=3$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}(-3) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-3,-2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=3.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}x \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3}(3) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(3,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1+\\frac{1}{3}x.$\n\n![](images/6b/6bb16e235d5a29fb9c99c91a7af7ef7af5cdec31ed7d091167d30d993b5ff511.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186702944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07500677-4006-5031-8984-9c407955cae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9aa24d423a53853a992fdc06